---
<Base title="Try the lab — Desktop Lab" description="Start the prepared VMs, test Lucent’s launcher and widgets, build the Rust workspace, understand service ownership and roll back safely.">
  <div class="wrap guide-intro"><a class="text-link" href="/">← Back to the desktop</a><span class="eyebrow">THE HANDS-ON GUIDE</span><h1>From reading<br />to running.</h1><p>The prepared VM is the shortest route. Building on an existing Omarchy installation is the next. Here’s how to try both, and how to get back.</p></div>
  <div class="wrap guide-layout">
    <aside class="guide-nav"><span class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</span><nav aria-label="Guide contents"><a href="#start">01 &nbsp; Start a VM</a><a href="#interactions">02 &nbsp; Try the interactions</a><a href="#ownership">03 &nbsp; Understand the pieces</a><a href="#build">04 &nbsp; Build & install</a><a href="#state">05 &nbsp; State & services</a><a href="#rollback">06 &nbsp; Roll back</a><a href="#validation">07 &nbsp; Verify it yourself</a><a href="#credits">08 &nbsp; Sources & credits</a></nav></aside>
    <div class="guide-content">
      <section id="start"><span class="eyebrow">01 / PREPARED LAB HOST</span><h2>Start one experiment.</h2><p>From the repository root on the host where the lab VMs were prepared, run:</p><CodeBlock code="python3 vm/launch.py lucent" label="Host · open the Lucent VM" /><p>This starts the existing guest and opens its console. The guests appear in Virtual Machine Manager under <strong>QEMU/KVM User session</strong>. The launcher expects the local VM inventory and disks; cloning the repository alone does not create a ready-to-boot VM.</p><p>Lucid and Noctalia are separate guests. Shut down the current guest before opening the next:</p><CodeBlock code={`python3 vm/lab.py shutdown lucent\npython3 vm/launch.py lucid\n# Later, switch to the C++ comparison:\npython3 vm/lab.py shutdown lucid\npython3 vm/launch.py noctalia`} label="Host · switch experiments" /><div class="callout"><strong>Login and local access</strong><p>Use the source Omarchy VM’s existing guest login. Credentials stay in its local login note and are not in this site or Git. SSH is loopback-only: Lucid 2241, Noctalia 2242, Lucent 2243.</p></div><p>Starting from scratch? Follow the repository’s <a href={`${repo}docs/install-and-rollback.md`}>OS installation and VM preparation guide</a>. Disk selection and any destructive OS installation steps remain manual.</p></section>
      <section id="interactions"><span class="eyebrow">02 / INSIDE THE LUCENT GUEST</span><h2>Take the desktop for a spin.</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Lucent shortcuts"><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody>{shortcuts.map(([key, result]) => <tr><td><kbd>{key}</kbd></td><td>{result}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h3>A useful first five minutes</h3><ol class="guide-steps"><li><strong>Launch a terminal.</strong> Press Super + Space, search for Foot, and press Enter. It opens a real application window.</li><li><strong>Try the tiling.</strong> Open another terminal. Hyprland manages ordinary windows with the existing Omarchy bindings. Lucent’s widgets stay outside that tiling layout.</li><li><strong>Move a widget.</strong> Drag the calendar’s background and release. Restart Lucent and the position should return.</li><li><strong>Change the background.</strong> Open the wallpaper carousel, select with the arrows and press Enter. This calls Omarchy’s background command.</li><li><strong>Explore the selectors.</strong> Toggle widgets, reset their positions, or select the light/dark palette in the launcher header.</li></ol><p>The seven available widgets are calendar, clock, weather, media, system monitor, notes and focus timer. Notes currently support one line. The timer supports start, pause and reset; its running state is not persisted across restart.</p><h3>Control it from a terminal</h3><CodeBlock code={`lucent-cli launcher toggle\nlucent-cli wallpapers open\nlucent-cli widgets open\nlucent-cli inspect\nlucent-cli quit`} label="Guest · Lucent IPC" /><p><code>inspect</code> includes geometry and frame counters, as well as the current query and note. Review that personal content before sharing output. <code>quit</code> stops Lucent and restores the stock bar.</p></section>
      <section id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">03 / WHO DOES WHAT?</span><h2>The pieces fit at their boundaries.</h2><div class="faq-list"><details open><summary>What is actually custom in Lucent?</summary><p>The Rust framework, renderer, native surface runtime, domain models, use cases and desktop client. The visible bar, dock, launcher, wallpaper/widget selectors, seven widgets, notifications and lock/login screens are ours. Established libraries provide Wayland bindings and GPU access.</p></details><details><summary>Why does Lucent still feel like Omarchy?</summary><p>It runs inside the existing Omarchy session. Hyprland still controls tiling, workspaces and app windows. Omarchy still supplies the background surface, polkit, idle handling and other session services. Lucent takes over the bar and notification service after its surfaces are ready. Lock requests use our secure native locker, with the stock locker retained for recovery. The separate Lucent greeter runs under greetd.</p></details><details><summary>Why can Aether and other apps appear in every launcher?</summary><p>The desktops share the installed applications cloned from the same base VM. A launcher discovers application entries and asks the operating system to start them. An application does not have to use the shell’s UI toolkit. The same app can be launched from Lucid, Noctalia or Lucent.</p></details><details><summary>Are widgets normal windows? Can they be dragged?</summary><p>They belong to a Wayland bottom-layer surface, with input regions matching the interactive cards. Dragging changes a widget’s position within that surface and saves it on release. They stay below application windows and do not become tiling windows. Resizing is not implemented yet.</p></details><details><summary>Does it really use Vulkan?</summary><p>Yes. wgpu uses Vulkan for the native renderer, with one shared device and resource cache across surfaces. In the prepared VM the Vulkan implementation is Mesa lavapipe, which renders on the CPU. Hardware GPU performance has not been measured.</p></details><details><summary>Is this a complete copy of Lucid?</summary><p>It follows the reference layout, palette, typography and primary dock/launcher/selector transitions. Full feature and pixel parity are still incomplete. Tray hosting, clipboard/emoji modes, full settings and control-center panels, automatic wallpaper palettes and several platform features remain future work.</p></details><details><summary>Where did the original Rust demo card go?</summary><p>The desktop client now has three persistent surfaces plus an on-demand notification surface. The independent <code>hello-layer</code> example is still available to demonstrate child components, typed effects, motion and the native renderer without the full desktop.</p><CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --release -p hello-layer" label="Guest · independent framework example" /></details></div></section>
      <section id="build"><span class="eyebrow">04 / EXISTING OMARCHY INSTALLATION</span><h2>Build the native client.</h2><p>The tested baseline is Omarchy 4.0.4 with Hyprland 0.56.2 and Lua configuration. These integration scripts target Omarchy; they are not a generic installer for an arbitrary Linux session.</p><p>Arch build/runtime packages: <code>rust</code>, <code>pkgconf</code>, <code>wayland</code>, <code>libxkbcommon</code>, <code>fontconfig</code>, <code>pam</code>, <code>clang</code>, <code>vulkan-icd-loader</code> and a suitable Vulkan driver. Desktop adapters use <code>playerctl</code>, <code>wireplumber</code>, <code>networkmanager</code> and <code>curl</code>.</p><CodeBlock code={`cargo build --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --release --workspace\npython3 scripts/lucent-setup.py install\nsystemctl --user start lucent.service`} label="Omarchy guest · build and try" /><p>Check the running client first. Then enable its managed startup and shortcut blocks:</p><CodeBlock code="python3 scripts/lucent-setup.py activate" label="Omarchy guest · activate" /><p>Installation is per-user. Activation edits marked blocks in user <code>bindings.lua</code> and <code>autostart.lua</code>. Packaged files under <code>/usr/share/omarchy</code> are untouched. The service hands off notifications using Omarchy’s guarded restart while unlocked, then restores the stock notification plugin and bar on stop/failure.</p></section>
      <section id="state"><span class="eyebrow">05 / SETTINGS & SERVICES</span><h2>Predictable state, real services.</h2><dl class="guide-definitions"><div><dt>Layout and preferences</dt><dd><code>~/.local/state/lucent/desktop.json</code> stores versioned widget positions, visibility, pinned apps, notes and palette choice. Saves are atomic. Unsupported versions are left intact and saving is disabled until corrected.</dd></div><div><dt>Wallpapers</dt><dd>Place images in <code>~/Pictures/Wallpapers</code> or <code>~/.config/lucent/wallpapers</code>. Applying an image delegates to <code>omarchy-theme-bg-set</code>. The dark/light palette currently uses shared reference colors; it does not extract a new palette from each wallpaper.</dd></div><div><dt>Weather</dt><dd>Optional configuration in <code>~/.config/lucent/weather.json</code> takes numeric <code>latitude</code> and <code>longitude</code>. Open-Meteo is queried every 15 minutes. There is no IP lookup. Keep personal location files outside Git.</dd></div><div><dt>External integrations</dt><dd>Hyprland changes arrive through its event socket. Current clock, system and media adapters poll at 1, 5 and 2 seconds respectively. Only visible data changes invalidate surfaces. CLI service adapters are an initial implementation; native D-Bus subscriptions remain future work.</dd></div><div><dt>Application execution</dt><dd>XDG desktop entries are parsed into executable and argument arrays, with supported field codes handled explicitly. Commands are not evaluated through a shell.</dd></div><div><dt>Local IPC</dt><dd>A mode-0600 socket lives at <code>$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lucent.sock</code>. Commands use an allowlist; a file lock prevents duplicate clients.</dd></div></dl></section>
      <section id="session-ui"><span class="eyebrow">NATIVE SESSION UI</span><h2>Lock, notifications, login.</h2><p>The bar’s bell opens Lucent’s notification history. The lock button and Super+Ctrl+L request its native secure locker. Wayland enforces the lock; the existing Omarchy PAM policy verifies your password. The login greeter is another client of the same component API and Vulkan renderer, with greetd managing authentication and session startup.</p><CodeBlock code="sudo pacman -S --needed greetd
sudo python3 scripts/lucent-login.py install
sudo python3 scripts/lucent-login.py test
# After checking VT9, stop the test:
sudo systemctl stop lucent-greeter-test
sudo python3 scripts/lucent-login.py activate" label="Guest · test and select the native greeter for next boot" /><p>Activation preserves the current desktop. To restore the previous display manager for next boot, run <code>sudo python3 scripts/lucent-login.py rollback</code>. The installer records its original selection and greetd configuration.</p></section><section id="rollback"><span class="eyebrow">06 / BACK TO STOCK</span><h2>A way back is part of the design.</h2><h3>Lucent</h3><CodeBlock code="python3 scripts/lucent-setup.py rollback" label="Guest · restore stock integration" /><p>Rollback stops Lucent, removes the managed blocks and restores stock shortcuts and bar visibility. Installed binaries, widget settings and the selected wallpaper remain. For the prepared VM’s original wallpaper:</p><CodeBlock code={'omarchy-theme-bg-set "$(cat ~/.config/lucent/original-wallpaper)"'} label="Guest · restore the original wallpaper" /><h3>Lucid or Noctalia</h3><CodeBlock code="~/desktop-lab/scripts/rollback.sh" label="Replacement-shell guest · rollback" /><p>Log out and back in afterward. The original user configuration is restored; packages remain installed and their before/after inventories remain available for review.</p><div class="callout"><strong>After an Omarchy update</strong><p>The Lucid/Noctalia integration checks the packaged startup checksum. If it changes, rerun the doctor, review the diff, and stage a fresh override before the next login. Compatibility is verified against the installed baseline.</p></div></section>
      <section id="validation"><span class="eyebrow">07 / REPRODUCE THE EVIDENCE</span><h2>Run the checks.</h2><CodeBlock code={`cargo fmt --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --all --check\ncargo clippy --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --workspace\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v`} label="Repository · code and integration checks" /><p>The VM harness sends real QEMU pointer and keyboard events. Run it from the prepared host with the Lucent guest unlocked at 1920 × 1080:</p><CodeBlock code={`python3 vm/test-framework.py\npython3 vm/test-launcher.py\npython3 vm/test-visual.py`} label="Host · real-input checks and performance" /><p>The input harness preserves the original widget settings and wallpaper. The measurement tool temporarily uses Omarchy’s supported stay-awake state so the screensaver cannot obscure the surfaces, then restores it.</p><p>The recorded results cover 40 Rust unit tests and 60 native Vulkan visual snapshots, 16 configuration/token/architecture tests, plus actual VM interactions. The full <a href={`${repo}reports/lucent-framework.md`}>validation report</a> separates executed checks from untested hardware paths. <a href="/reports/performance.json">Download the recorded performance JSON</a>.</p></section>
      <section id="credits"><span class="eyebrow">08 / REFERENCES & ATTRIBUTION</span><h2>Built with a clear lineage.</h2><ul class="resource-list"><li><a href="https://github.com/omacom/omarchy">Omarchy ↗</a><span>The Arch / Hyprland foundation and retained session tools.</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Sn3akyy1/lucid">Lucid ↗</a><span>Primary visual and interaction reference. The inspected upstream revision is pinned in the lab manifest.</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.noctalia.dev/noctalia/">Noctalia v5 ↗</a><span>The native C++ comparison, configured through its supported schema.</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/MystiaFin/amane">Amane ↗</a><span>Architectural research into immediate-mode native desktop shells.</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/MystiaFin/suzuha">Suzuha ↗</a><span>Reference client studied alongside Amane.</span></li><li><a href={`${repo}docs/adr/0002-framework-client.md`}>Architecture decisions ↗</a><span>Why this project uses an independent framework and client.</span></li><li><a href={`${repo}manifests/upstream-lock.json`}>Pinned sources ↗</a><span>Inspected revisions and archive checksums.</span></li></ul><p>Screenshots are actual captures from the lab. Wallpapers retain their original rights and are shown as part of those desktop captures. The site reuses the client’s Lucent Sans and Lucent Display fonts, derived from Google Sans Flex under the <a href="/licenses/fonts.txt">SIL Open Font License</a>. See the repository’s <a href={`${repo}lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/assets/README.md`}>asset attribution</a>.</p><p>This explanatory site is built with <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> and small browser scripts. The desktop it documents is a native Rust application.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
